WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.708 --> 00:00:06.628
A few days ago, I flew in from Chicago.

00:00:06.628 --> 00:00:08.713
I love it there.

00:00:08.755 --> 00:00:10.090
Chicago has it all.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:11.466
Oh, thank you.

00:00:11.466 --> 00:00:13.134
Chicago has it all.

00:00:13.176 --> 00:00:17.389
Great food, incredible skyline
and architecture

00:00:17.389 --> 00:00:20.809
and the kindest Midwestern folks
you'll ever meet.

00:00:21.351 --> 00:00:24.896
Diversity of people
and culture, second to none.

00:00:25.564 --> 00:00:29.609
Millions of people move
to cities like Chicago each year

00:00:29.651 --> 00:00:32.153
to pursue their life's
dreams and passions.

00:00:32.153 --> 00:00:33.321
I know I did.

00:00:33.613 --> 00:00:39.953
And that's because cities are booming
marketplaces of people and companies,

00:00:39.995 --> 00:00:42.038
of products and materials

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.000
and something not so great:

00:00:45.041 --> 00:00:48.128
waste and greenhouse gas emissions.

00:00:48.712 --> 00:00:52.424
According to a 2022 World Bank report,

00:00:52.424 --> 00:00:54.759
our beloved communities
could be responsible

00:00:54.759 --> 00:00:58.972
for 25 gigatons of carbon emissions

00:00:59.014 --> 00:01:01.850
and a ton and ton of waste.

00:01:02.475 --> 00:01:04.519
But what does this mean
for your community?

00:01:04.936 --> 00:01:07.355
Think about that building being torn down,

00:01:07.397 --> 00:01:09.858
maybe on the corner
next to your office or your home

00:01:09.858 --> 00:01:12.736
and all the wood and concrete and steel

00:01:12.777 --> 00:01:15.238
or the office equipment or computers

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:19.492
or furniture being tossed out,
dumped in a landfill,

00:01:19.492 --> 00:01:23.079
often near the homes
of our low-income neighbors.

00:01:23.538 --> 00:01:25.081
In the United States,

00:01:25.081 --> 00:01:30.211
40 percent of landfill contain materials
from the construction

00:01:30.211 --> 00:01:32.047
and demolition of buildings.

00:01:33.006 --> 00:01:35.925
This is our linear economy.

00:01:36.301 --> 00:01:37.761
It's bad for our climate,

00:01:37.761 --> 00:01:41.556
it's bad for our health,
and it's a missed economic opportunity.

00:01:42.307 --> 00:01:46.770
But we can turn this line into a circle.

00:01:47.187 --> 00:01:49.606
Many connected circles, in fact,

00:01:49.606 --> 00:01:53.818
where every a physical resource
finds another use and another life,

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:57.405
driving down greenhouse gas emissions
from new manufacturing,

00:01:57.447 --> 00:02:01.201
shrinking harmful landfill
and creating a lot of jobs.

00:02:02.118 --> 00:02:05.413
This disconnected linear economy

00:02:05.413 --> 00:02:07.332
becomes a circular one

00:02:07.374 --> 00:02:11.127
when we transform our concept of ownership

00:02:11.169 --> 00:02:14.464
into a system where every person
and every business

00:02:14.506 --> 00:02:19.302
has access to the things
others no longer find useful.

00:02:19.928 --> 00:02:24.849
Wood from that building being torn down
could be a dining room table.

00:02:24.891 --> 00:02:27.644
Bricks could be concrete again

00:02:27.644 --> 00:02:29.729
and furniture can be rehabbed

00:02:29.771 --> 00:02:32.107
and resold to an up-and-coming start-up.

00:02:33.274 --> 00:02:37.696
Now my circular economy journey
started on a pretty small scale.

00:02:37.737 --> 00:02:41.825
I was a neuroscientist in a lab
at Northwestern University.

00:02:43.451 --> 00:02:46.037
At that time, in my lab,
there were hundreds

00:02:46.079 --> 00:02:48.623
if not thousands of biological tools

00:02:48.623 --> 00:02:52.502
and things like microscopes,
that were going completely unused.

00:02:53.378 --> 00:02:57.882
In fact, research budgets
were tight then and actually now,

00:02:57.924 --> 00:03:00.719
but still, these things
sat around collecting dust.

00:03:00.719 --> 00:03:02.929
No one even knew they existed.

00:03:03.304 --> 00:03:06.099
So I loaded up a push cart,

00:03:06.099 --> 00:03:08.351
rolled it around my floor
with these items,

00:03:08.351 --> 00:03:11.521
visited my colleagues,
shared what we no longer needed

00:03:11.521 --> 00:03:13.314
and they might be able to.

00:03:13.898 --> 00:03:17.152
Rheaply, the company I founded and run,

00:03:17.152 --> 00:03:21.614
helps organizations identify
and catalog the things they own,

00:03:21.656 --> 00:03:24.784
reuse them internally when they can

00:03:24.826 --> 00:03:28.121
and distribute them to other
organizations when they cannot.

00:03:28.538 --> 00:03:33.960
It's like a high-tech, scaled-up version
of me with the push cart at Northwestern,

00:03:34.002 --> 00:03:36.337
but with hundreds of organizations,

00:03:36.379 --> 00:03:39.007
from technology to manufacturing leaders,

00:03:39.048 --> 00:03:43.386
and millions of items
available for ale and donation --

00:03:43.386 --> 00:03:46.097
all cataloged on a digital platform,

00:03:46.097 --> 00:03:49.809
stretching across partners
in a local area, a city or state,

00:03:49.809 --> 00:03:52.896
creating connected networks of reuse.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:57.025
Rheaply helps organizations reuse
things like building materials

00:03:57.025 --> 00:04:00.111
and IT and industrial
equipment and furniture.

00:04:00.987 --> 00:04:05.658
But companies like Goodr or Olio
help reduce food waste

00:04:05.658 --> 00:04:08.828
by connecting surplus food
to people who are hungry.

00:04:09.537 --> 00:04:13.500
And companies like Queen of Raw
and Trove and Recurate

00:04:13.541 --> 00:04:16.294
create circular loops for textiles,

00:04:16.336 --> 00:04:18.254
apparel and other branded goods.

00:04:19.631 --> 00:04:22.217
Let's take a look
at how it works for furniture.

00:04:22.801 --> 00:04:28.640
An investment bank in New York City
had about 2,000 premium office chairs

00:04:28.681 --> 00:04:30.350
that they no longer could use.

00:04:30.809 --> 00:04:31.976
When they hired us,

00:04:32.018 --> 00:04:35.230
we sent in a team to inventory
that furniture on that floor.

00:04:36.731 --> 00:04:40.777
We photographed it, we tagged it
and we uploaded it to the system.

00:04:42.362 --> 00:04:47.534
When it was determined that these items
could no longer be useful internally,

00:04:47.575 --> 00:04:51.621
a notification was sent out through
our platform to all of our partners,

00:04:51.621 --> 00:04:53.248
hundreds of them.

00:04:53.748 --> 00:04:57.293
I'm happy to report that all
of these chairs found new homes

00:04:57.293 --> 00:04:58.753
in a local university,

00:04:58.795 --> 00:05:00.505
a community housing organization

00:05:00.547 --> 00:05:02.799
and a local refurbishment company.

00:05:04.717 --> 00:05:05.885
Thank you.

00:05:05.885 --> 00:05:08.721
(Applause)

00:05:09.514 --> 00:05:14.269
In total, about 68,000 pounds
of potential waste

00:05:14.269 --> 00:05:16.020
was diverted from landfill,

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:17.981
and about 100,000 dollars of value

00:05:17.981 --> 00:05:21.067
was recaptured and shared
with these organizations.

00:05:21.109 --> 00:05:23.319
Not to mention the carbon savings.

00:05:23.361 --> 00:05:28.616
Making one of these office chairs,
which weighs about 55 pounds,

00:05:28.616 --> 00:05:34.080
releases 245 pounds of carbon
emissions into the atmosphere.

00:05:34.706 --> 00:05:38.459
Now, this is just a small set
of furniture on one floor

00:05:38.459 --> 00:05:41.296
in one building of one bank
in New York City.

00:05:41.921 --> 00:05:45.466
Think of all the other office equipment
and IT infrastructure

00:05:45.466 --> 00:05:46.926
that we could be reusing.

00:05:47.719 --> 00:05:49.137
In this specific case,

00:05:49.178 --> 00:05:52.265
the bank paid us as a part of meeting
their sustainability goals,

00:05:52.265 --> 00:05:54.225
but the chairs were mostly donated.

00:05:54.893 --> 00:06:00.690
Sometimes furniture or equipment
or materials can be resold

00:06:00.732 --> 00:06:03.192
even for a 50-plus percent discount.

00:06:03.192 --> 00:06:07.614
Sometimes refurbished
and sometimes split into parts

00:06:07.614 --> 00:06:09.699
and used by industrial recycler.

00:06:10.408 --> 00:06:11.618
OK.

00:06:11.659 --> 00:06:14.120
So we know how to create
a circular economy

00:06:14.162 --> 00:06:17.332
with a microscope on a pushcart
like me at Northwestern.

00:06:19.083 --> 00:06:23.171
And we know how to do so

00:06:23.212 --> 00:06:26.966
inside and outside of a building
with either its building materials

00:06:27.008 --> 00:06:31.095
or things like IT infrastructure
on a digital platform like Rheaply.

00:06:32.889 --> 00:06:36.643
But how do we get to the scale of a city?

00:06:39.103 --> 00:06:40.396
And why?

00:06:41.397 --> 00:06:48.071
Well, cities occupy about three percent
of global land mass

00:06:48.112 --> 00:06:51.991
but house over 50 percent
of the global population,

00:06:52.033 --> 00:06:56.829
which commands over 75 percent
of all global resources.

00:06:57.205 --> 00:07:00.625
Cities are perfect, tractable fronts

00:07:00.625 --> 00:07:03.127
for us to drive down
greenhouse gas emissions

00:07:03.127 --> 00:07:08.675
and create a lot of jobs
by building local circular economies.

00:07:09.968 --> 00:07:14.639
Now to transition any city's linear
economy to a circular one,

00:07:14.639 --> 00:07:17.809
we're going to, at the very least,
have to do three things.

00:07:18.142 --> 00:07:22.021
First, we have to build
a digital infrastructure

00:07:22.021 --> 00:07:24.565
to connect every citizen
and every company

00:07:24.607 --> 00:07:26.818
to everything in their city.

00:07:27.443 --> 00:07:30.863
Next, we're going to have to build
the operational infrastructure

00:07:30.863 --> 00:07:34.867
to make reuse and recycling
and remanufacturing easy

00:07:34.867 --> 00:07:36.661
and universally acceptable.

00:07:38.538 --> 00:07:43.334
Next, we're going to have to incentivize
every person and business

00:07:43.334 --> 00:07:45.878
to participate in their
local circular economy.

00:07:47.088 --> 00:07:49.507
As you might expect from a person like me,

00:07:49.507 --> 00:07:53.094
technology is at the heart
of any circular city.

00:07:53.136 --> 00:07:56.014
So at the very first step,

00:07:56.055 --> 00:07:59.308
we have to provide
universal access to the internet.

00:07:59.767 --> 00:08:02.103
But then very quickly after,

00:08:02.103 --> 00:08:04.647
we have to create local marketplaces,

00:08:04.647 --> 00:08:06.691
collections of digital platforms

00:08:06.691 --> 00:08:11.404
where anything from toys
to building materials can be posted,

00:08:11.404 --> 00:08:13.448
found, collected and exchanged.

00:08:13.781 --> 00:08:16.576
Now, this is already happening
on platforms like Craigslist

00:08:16.576 --> 00:08:19.120
and Facebook and the Buy Nothing Project,

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:23.541
but we have to go much bigger and expand
into many more product categories.

00:08:23.958 --> 00:08:27.086
In fact, we’re going
to have to like, Google Map

00:08:27.086 --> 00:08:31.841
all the industrial equipment
to office furniture in our cities.

00:08:31.883 --> 00:08:33.259
Because in a circular city,

00:08:33.301 --> 00:08:36.012
we actually have to know where things are.

00:08:36.012 --> 00:08:37.555
But if we can do this,

00:08:37.597 --> 00:08:39.474
we create a second and third life

00:08:39.474 --> 00:08:41.893
for every physical resource,

00:08:41.934 --> 00:08:46.481
increasing its lifetime value
and delaying its journey to the landfill.

00:08:47.815 --> 00:08:50.234
But it's not just about reusing things.

00:08:50.735 --> 00:08:53.821
If we're going to sustain
this connected flow

00:08:53.863 --> 00:08:56.199
of circular products and materials,

00:08:56.199 --> 00:08:58.701
we're going to have to build
the operational capacity

00:08:58.701 --> 00:09:01.162
in recycling, remanufacturing and reuse.

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:05.416
And we're going to need things
like repair shops

00:09:05.458 --> 00:09:09.670
and training programs
like Arne Duncan’s Chicago CRED,

00:09:09.712 --> 00:09:13.382
where they're upskilling at-risk youth
on the South Side of Chicago.

00:09:13.424 --> 00:09:17.220
Or the circular construction laboratory
at Cornell University,

00:09:17.261 --> 00:09:21.557
where they're training the next generation
of eco-conscious architects.

00:09:22.016 --> 00:09:25.937
With greater capacity,
we can reduce the green premium

00:09:25.937 --> 00:09:28.856
or cost associated
with reuse and recycling.

00:09:29.524 --> 00:09:31.734
And we're going to need more local hubs

00:09:31.734 --> 00:09:34.695
like the Lifecycle Building
Center in Atlanta,

00:09:34.737 --> 00:09:38.950
where building materials can be donated
and reused in the community.

00:09:38.991 --> 00:09:41.786
And we’re going to need
more refurbishment companies

00:09:41.828 --> 00:09:46.249
like The Furniture X-Change in New Jersey
or Envirotech in Ontario,

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:48.126
where they take old furniture

00:09:48.167 --> 00:09:50.670
and make something modern and appealing.

00:09:50.711 --> 00:09:54.632
And of course, we're going to need more
local shipping and delivery companies.

00:09:55.508 --> 00:09:59.053
Last but very importantly,

00:09:59.053 --> 00:10:03.349
we're going to have to match these digital
and operational infrastructure investments

00:10:03.391 --> 00:10:05.434
with smart public policy

00:10:05.434 --> 00:10:09.147
that incentivizes participation
in a circular economic model.

00:10:09.564 --> 00:10:10.731
In San Francisco,

00:10:10.731 --> 00:10:14.152
the Department of Environment
has adopted ordinances

00:10:14.193 --> 00:10:17.864
and solutions that drive
local circularity and reuse.

00:10:18.281 --> 00:10:19.532
Last November,

00:10:19.574 --> 00:10:22.535
Massachusetts made it illegal
to throw out textiles,

00:10:22.577 --> 00:10:24.662
becoming the very first state to do so.

00:10:24.996 --> 00:10:29.208
They've even launched a website
that maps out all the recycling outlets

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:31.252
where these materials can be discarded.

00:10:31.669 --> 00:10:36.299
And in San Antonio,
one of my personal favorites,

00:10:36.299 --> 00:10:39.844
you don't pay property taxes
for five years

00:10:39.844 --> 00:10:43.181
if you rehab a building
in a local historic district.

00:10:44.265 --> 00:10:47.268
Now, here's where it's exciting
for public policymakers.

00:10:47.643 --> 00:10:50.730
A circular city could be
a thriving economy.

00:10:51.063 --> 00:10:53.941
Businesses in these
city-based circular economies

00:10:53.983 --> 00:10:58.613
are going to need large local workforces
to update internet infrastructure,

00:10:58.654 --> 00:11:01.157
to repair or remanufacture goods

00:11:01.199 --> 00:11:04.410
or just to resell and drive
things across town.

00:11:05.661 --> 00:11:09.081
Now these ideas
are getting a lot of traction.

00:11:09.081 --> 00:11:13.127
Last year, Rheaply,
with many other organizations,

00:11:13.169 --> 00:11:17.590
formed the Circular City Coalition
led by Pyxera Global.

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:21.677
The Coalition's purpose
is to help any city transition

00:11:21.677 --> 00:11:23.846
from linear to circular,

00:11:23.888 --> 00:11:27.475
while making sure it benefits everyone
in the community equally.

00:11:28.935 --> 00:11:31.145
And here's where I get excited,

00:11:31.187 --> 00:11:33.648
and I think you all should, too.

00:11:33.689 --> 00:11:37.318
If we can summon the focus and energy

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:43.074
to build just 1,000
of these circular cities by 2040,

00:11:43.115 --> 00:11:48.412
we'll drive down global greenhouse
gas emissions by more than 60 percent

00:11:48.454 --> 00:11:50.790
and get back on track
to meeting the targets

00:11:50.831 --> 00:11:52.833
set out in the Paris Accords.

00:11:55.586 --> 00:11:56.796
Yes.

00:11:56.837 --> 00:11:59.006
(Applause)

00:11:59.006 --> 00:12:00.508
We've got to do it.

00:12:01.259 --> 00:12:03.511
I'm a city boy from Chicago.

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:06.973
And I know you love your city, too.

00:12:07.556 --> 00:12:12.937
Cities are ground zero in the fight
against global climate change.

00:12:13.604 --> 00:12:17.566
And in that fight, we're all neighbors,

00:12:17.566 --> 00:12:20.528
not competitors, not strangers.

00:12:21.028 --> 00:12:22.405
We need each other

00:12:22.446 --> 00:12:24.991
in whatever city or town we reside in.

00:12:25.783 --> 00:12:31.414
And if we can all just participate
in making our cities circular,

00:12:31.455 --> 00:12:35.710
sharing resources locally
when we no longer need them,

00:12:35.751 --> 00:12:38.796
a concept that simple,

00:12:38.838 --> 00:12:41.257
then a net-zero future is possible.

00:12:41.257 --> 00:12:42.675
Thank you.

00:12:42.717 --> 00:12:43.843
Thank you, TED.

00:12:43.884 --> 00:12:47.388
(Applause)

